import{_ as t,o as s,c as a,a2 as i}from"./chunks/framework.D66Xq0Kv.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Install","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/install.md","filePath":"guide/install.md"}'),n={name:"guide/install.md"};function o(r,e,l,h,d,c){return s(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i(`<h1 id="install" tabindex="-1">Install <a class="header-anchor" href="#install" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Install&quot;">​</a></h1><p>From a clean server to an application answering on HTTPS.</p><h2 id="requirements" tabindex="-1">Requirements <a class="header-anchor" href="#requirements" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Requirements {#requirements}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>OS</th><th>Architecture</th><th>How it is verified</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ubuntu 26.04</td><td>x86_64</td><td>Checked by hand on a reference VPS; the installer also runs in an Ubuntu 26.04 container</td></tr><tr><td>Ubuntu 24.04</td><td>x86_64, arm64</td><td>Real installations in automated tests, including an interruption and resume</td></tr><tr><td>Ubuntu 22.04</td><td>x86_64</td><td>Real installations in automated tests, as above</td></tr><tr><td>Ubuntu 22.04</td><td>arm64</td><td>Declared; checked by hand only</td></tr><tr><td>Debian 12</td><td>x86_64, arm64</td><td>Declared; checked by hand only</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Orbit&#39;s checks refuse anything else. The server also needs:</p><ul><li>2 vCPUs, 2 GB of memory and 20 GB free under <code>/var/lib</code>. 4 GB is better if you build Next.js or Nuxt applications on it.</li><li>Root over SSH, a synchronized clock and working DNS.</li><li>Outbound HTTPS to github.com, release-assets.githubusercontent.com and quay.io.</li><li>Ports 80, 443 and 6443 free. Do not use an image with a control panel or a web server.</li><li>If your provider has a firewall, allow 22, 80 and 443. Keep 6443 and 7443 closed to the internet; open them only to the <a href="./servers">servers you add later</a>.</li><li>Some providers give the server only an address inside their network and map a public one to it: Oracle Cloud, AWS, Google Cloud and Azure. Orbit notices it, asks checkip.amazonaws.com or api.ipify.org for the public address and uses that one for the console, generated hostnames and DNS records. On Oracle Cloud, ports 80 and 443 are opened in the subnet&#39;s security list.</li></ul><h2 id="installer" tabindex="-1">Running the installer <a class="header-anchor" href="#installer" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Running the installer {#installer}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On the server:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">curl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -fsSL</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> https://github.com/getcortexlabs/orbit-releases/releases/latest/download/install.sh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> sudo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> sh</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The installer:</p><ol><li>checks that it runs as root on Linux with systemd, on x86_64 or arm64;</li><li>downloads Orbit for the server&#39;s architecture from <a href="https://github.com/getcortexlabs/orbit-releases/releases" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">the releases</a> and refuses an archive whose SHA-256 does not match (<code>ORBIT_VERSION=0.1.27</code> pins a version);</li><li>starts the service and checks the machine. If a check blocks, it stops before changing anything and says how to fix it;</li><li>installs K3s, Traefik, cert-manager, BuildKit and the Orbit agent, in about five minutes;</li><li>ends with the console&#39;s address and a one-time token:</li></ol><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Orbit is ready.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  Console  https://orbit.203-0-113-10.sslip.io</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  Token    boot-7KQ2-MX9P-4TRW</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Running the same command again resumes an interrupted installation or updates Orbit.</p><p>If an earlier version published the console on an address like <code>orbit.10-0-0-5.sslip.io</code>, updating moves the console and every generated hostname to the public address. <code>orbit status</code> prints the new one.</p><h2 id="first-steps" tabindex="-1">First steps <a class="header-anchor" href="#first-steps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;First steps {#first-steps}&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>Open the console.</strong> Open the address and create the administrator with the token. The certificate comes from Let&#39;s Encrypt, so the server must be reachable on port 80.</li><li><strong>Connect GitHub</strong> (optional). Under New project, choose Connect GitHub. GitHub creates an App for this Orbit and asks where to install it and which repositories it may read. See <a href="./github">GitHub and previews</a>. To build from GitLab or Bitbucket instead, connect it under Settings → Integrations with an access token; see <a href="./gitlab">GitLab</a> and <a href="./bitbucket">Bitbucket</a>.</li><li><strong>Deploy.</strong> Create a project from a repository or a container image. Orbit builds it on the server, gives it an HTTPS address and checks that it answers.</li><li><strong>Add a database</strong> to the project and connect it: the application receives <code>DATABASE_URL</code> in its variables.</li><li><strong>Set backups</strong> to external storage, so your data survives losing the server. See <a href="./backups">Backups and recovery</a>.</li></ol><h2 id="console-address" tabindex="-1">The console&#39;s address <a class="header-anchor" href="#console-address" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The console&#39;s address {#console-address}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The console starts at <code>https://orbit.&lt;server-ip-with-dashes&gt;.sslip.io</code>. sslip.io is a public DNS service that resolves any name carrying an IP to that IP, so Orbit gets a certificate in the first minute without a domain of yours. To use your own, such as <code>orbit.example.com</code>, type it in <strong>Console address</strong> while installing, or later under <strong>Settings → Console address</strong>:</p><ol><li>Create the record Orbit shows: an <code>A</code> record pointing to the server&#39;s public address, or one per entry server when there are several. With <a href="./cloudflare">Cloudflare DNS connected</a> and the hostname in one of its zones, Orbit creates it.</li><li>Orbit checks DNS every 30 seconds, as it does for application domains, and says when the record is missing or points elsewhere.</li><li>Once it points here, Let&#39;s Encrypt signs a certificate, and the hostname becomes the console&#39;s address: links in notifications and the GitHub App&#39;s webhook move there, and GitLab and Bitbucket webhooks created from then on use it.</li></ol><p>The sslip.io address keeps answering the whole time, so a typo or a slow DNS change never locks you out. Once the new address has its certificate, <strong>Keep answering at</strong> turns the sslip.io address off; an empty hostname goes back to it. While a server runs <a href="./tunnel#console">Cloudflare Tunnel</a>, the hostname is published through the tunnel instead, with a proxied <code>CNAME</code>.</p><h2 id="what-it-adds" tabindex="-1">What the installation adds <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-it-adds" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the installation adds {#what-it-adds}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These services start at boot:</p><ul><li><code>orbit.service</code>, the control plane;</li><li><code>k3s.service</code>, Kubernetes;</li><li><code>orbit-firewall.service</code>, which keeps ports 6443 and 8080 reachable only from the machine and its workloads;</li><li><code>orbit-agent.service</code>, which reports the server&#39;s health to Orbit;</li><li><code>orbit-buildkit.service</code>, which builds your repositories.</li></ul><p>Orbit keeps its data in <code>/var/lib/orbit</code> and the agent&#39;s identity in <code>/var/lib/orbit-agent</code>.</p><h2 id="by-hand" tabindex="-1">Installing by hand <a class="header-anchor" href="#by-hand" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Installing by hand {#by-hand}&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><p>Download <code>orbit-linux-&lt;arch&gt;.tar.gz</code> from <a href="https://github.com/getcortexlabs/orbit-releases/releases" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">the releases</a>. It holds <code>orbit</code> (with the console inside), <code>orbit-agent</code> and <code>orbit.service</code>.</p></li><li><p>Copy them to the server and start the service:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">scp</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> orbit</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> orbit-agent</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> orbit.service</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> root@SERVER:/tmp/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">ssh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> root@SERVER</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;install -m 0755 /tmp/orbit /tmp/orbit-agent /usr/local/bin/ \\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">  &amp;&amp; install -m 0644 /tmp/orbit.service /etc/systemd/system/ \\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">  &amp;&amp; systemctl daemon-reload &amp;&amp; systemctl enable --now orbit&#39;</span></span></code></pre></div></li><li><p>Get the one-time token to create the administrator. It works once and expires after an hour:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">ssh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> root@SERVER</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> orbit</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> bootstrap-token</span></span></code></pre></div></li><li><p>Until the runtime is installed, the console only listens on <code>127.0.0.1:8080</code>. Open it through an SSH tunnel and browse to <a href="http://127.0.0.1:8080" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">http://127.0.0.1:8080</a>:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">ssh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -N</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -L</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> 8080:127.0.0.1:8080</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> root@SERVER</span></span></code></pre></div></li><li><p>Create the administrator and confirm the time zone the console suggests (see <a href="./time-zone">Time zone</a>), then follow the console&#39;s checks, plan and installation, or run <code>sudo orbit install</code> on the server.</p></li><li><p>Within a minute of the installation, the console is published at <code>https://orbit.&lt;server-ip-with-dashes&gt;.sslip.io</code> (<code>orbit status</code> prints it), with a Let&#39;s Encrypt certificate. The tunnel keeps working.</p></li></ol>`,26)])])}const b=t(n,[["render",o]]);export{u as __pageData,b as default};
